          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The lost sheep primarily signifies a sinner or spiritually straying person whom God seeks and restores; in passages such as Matthew 15:24 it specifically signifies straying or scattered Israel.\",\"book_summary\":\"The lost sheep primarily identifies the scattered house of Israel among the nations, sought for regathering, and more generally any unrepentant sinner.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"A person or people strayed from God, especially sinners generally, with Israel as a common passage-specific referent.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Strayed Israel specifically and, by generalization, any unrepentant sinner.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book reverses the usual order of emphasis by foregrounding scattered Israel and treating sinners generally as the broader application. Because both referents are expressly recognized by the consensus, this does not materially change the category or covered cases.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The consensus explicitly recognizes the lost sheep of Israel as a common interpretation, while the book also retains the consensus identification of the lost sheep as a sinner.\",\"rationale\":\"Reduced to their minimum identifications, both readings identify the lost sheep as those who have strayed from God, encompassing Israel in the specific Matthean context and sinners more generally. The book adds dispersion, mingling, and regathering language, but this elaborates its Israel-focused application rather than replacing the consensus referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 15:24 strongly supports identifying the lost sheep with the house of Israel, but the printed citation alone does not establish that they are specifically the scattered house mingled with the nations or explicitly describe their regathering; nor does it directly support the generalized reference to every unrepentant sinner.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
